const faqJsonLd = {"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the Jina AI API used for?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The Jina AI API is the production standard for the three building blocks of RAG: embedding (jina-embeddings-v3 turns text and images into a 1024-dim vector for vector search), reranking (jina-reranker-v3 re-orders the top-K results from the vector search before passing them to the LLM), and Reader (the URL-to-Markdown endpoint that fetches and cleans web content for embedding). The same Jina API key handles all three, which makes Jina the easiest production RAG stack to set up in 2026."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "How much does the Jina AI API cost?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Jina uses a flat per-million-token pricing model across all nine endpoints. jina-embeddings-v3 costs $0.02 per million tokens (10K free/month, permanent). jina-reranker-v3 costs $0.018 per million tokens. The Reader API costs $0.02 per million tokens (1M free/month). CLIP-v1 multimodal costs $0.02 per million tokens. With the 50% batch discount, the effective rate drops to $0.01 per million tokens, which is the cheapest production-quality embedding API in the market."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "Is there a Jina AI free tier?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes — Jina offers a permanent 10,000 tokens/month free tier on Embeddings, Reranker, and CLIP, plus 1M tokens/month free on the Reader API. The free tier does not require a credit card and does not expire. For prototypes and side projects under 10K tokens/month, the free tier is sufficient. For higher-volume use, the pre-paid credits start at $1, with auto-recharge at a $5 threshold by default."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I use Jina AI from inside China?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Jina's API is hosted on AWS US-East and EU-Frankfurt. Access from inside China requires a proxy, and the latency from a CN-based client is typically 200-400ms. For China-based production workloads, the recommended pattern is to use a Cloudflare Worker or a Tencent Cloud edge function as a proxy, which brings the latency down to 50-100ms and avoids the need for client-side proxy configuration. Note that the proxy does not bypass the content policy — Jina still blocks requests that violate the policy, regardless of geographic origin."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does Jina AI store my embeddings or text?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No — Jina does not retain input text or generated embeddings on the inference path. The text sent to the API is processed in memory, the embedding is returned, and the input is discarded. There is no logging of input text, no storage of generated embeddings, and no use of customer data for model training. The only data retained is the per-API-key usage telemetry (token count, request count, timestamp) for billing. The Reader API caches clean Markdown output for 1 hour per URL, but the cache is not used for training and can be bypassed with the x-no-cache: true header."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the difference between jina-embeddings-v3 and OpenAI text-embedding-3-large?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The three differences that matter for a production team: (1) jina-embeddings-v3 is 6.5x cheaper at the same 1024-dim output ($0.02/M vs $0.13/M tokens), (2) jina-embeddings-v3 has 8K context vs OpenAI's 8191 (effectively equivalent, but Jina's actual long-context performance is better in practice), (3) jina-embeddings-v3 ships with task-specific LoRA adapters (retrieval.query, retrieval.passage, classification, separation, text-matching) that improve accuracy by 4-7% nDCG@10 over single-mode embeddings. For multilingual RAG, jina-embeddings-v3 is the clear winner (100+ languages with explicit Chinese, German, and Code variants)."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is Matryoshka representation learning and why does it matter?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Matryoshka representation learning is a training technique that produces embeddings that can be truncated to a smaller dimension (e.g., 1024 to 256) with minimal accuracy loss. jina-embeddings-v3 is trained with Matryoshka, which means you can store the same vector at 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 768, or 1024 dimensions and trade recall for storage at query time without re-embedding your corpus. For a team storing 100M vectors, reducing the dimension from 1024 to 256 cuts the storage cost 4x and the retrieval latency 2-3x, at the cost of 1-3% recall. The same vector can be re-fetched at a higher dimension later if the recall is insufficient."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "How does the jina-reranker-v3 compare to Cohere Rerank-3.5?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "jina-reranker-v3 beats Cohere Rerank-3.5 by 6-11% nDCG@10 on Chinese/English mixed corpora and by 3-7% on European-language corpora. The price differential is even larger: jina-reranker-v3 at $0.018/M tokens is 111x cheaper than Cohere Rerank-3.5 at $2.00/M tokens. The two rerankers are equivalent on pure-English corpora, but Jina is the unambiguous winner for multilingual retrieval."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I self-host jina-embeddings on my own infrastructure?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The v2-base-* models (en, de, zh, code) are released under Apache 2.0 and can be self-hosted on a single A10 (24GB) or L4 GPU using the Hugging Face text-embeddings-inference container. The v3 model is not open-source as of July 2026; the only on-prem path is the Enterprise VPC contract. For a team embedding 50M tokens/month, self-hosting the v2-base model on a single L4 spot instance costs ~$80/month, which beats the Jina API cost ($1,000/month) by 12x — with the trade-off of self-host maintenance overhead."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does Jina have an MCP server?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes — Jina ships an MCP server at https://mcp.jina.ai/mcp that exposes the Reader, Embeddings, and Reranker endpoints as MCP tools for Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, and Continue. The MCP server is the easiest way to add web-grounded RAG to a Claude Code session — a single line in .claude/mcp.json wires up the Reader API, and any prompt that includes a URL gets the page content cleaned and embedded automatically."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is late_chunking in jina-embeddings-v3?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "late_chunking is a Jina-specific feature that applies the embedding model to the full document first, then chunks the resulting contextualized token embeddings, instead of the usual chunk first, embed second pipeline. This preserves long-range context across chunk boundaries and gives 5-9% nDCG@10 improvement on long-document benchmarks. For a team building RAG over PDFs longer than 20 pages, late_chunking is the single highest-ROI feature in the Jina SDK. Enable it with late_chunking=True on the embeddings.create() call."}},
  {"@type": "Question", "name": "How does Jina compare to OpenRouter for embeddings?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "OpenRouter is a router, not a primary provider — it fronts Jina, OpenAI, Cohere, Voyage, and Together AI behind a single API key, with a 0% cost markup. The use case for OpenRouter is when you want to A/B test different embedding providers without managing 5 separate API keys, or when you want a single bill across providers. For a team that has already picked Jina as the default, going through OpenRouter adds an extra hop on the inference path and hides the Matryoshka dimension switching, so direct Jina API is the right call."}}
]};

const enBody = `<h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Jina AI 2026: The Embedding, Reranker, and Reader API Stack for RAG</h1>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">If you have built a production RAG pipeline in 2025 or 2026, you have almost certainly evaluated Jina AI — the Berlin-based lab whose embeddings, reranker, and URL-to-Markdown Reader API have quietly become the de-facto trio for serious RAG workloads. The combination of an 8K-context embedding model with Matryoshka dimension reduction, a 100+ language reranker, and a one-line Reader that turns any web page into clean Markdown is the reason Jina sits in the default stack of LlamaIndex, LangChain, AutoGen, and Haystack — and the reason its API now processes more than a trillion tokens per month for production retrieval workloads.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">This review covers Jina AI from the perspective of an engineer evaluating it in July 2026: the nine production endpoints (Embeddings v3, four Embeddings v2 variants, CLIP-v1, Reranker v3, Reranker v2 multilingual, Reader), the per-million-token pricing model that makes Jina the cheapest production embedding API in the market, the Matryoshka dimension-switching trick that lets you trade accuracy for storage in a single API call, the multilingual retrieval story (which is the reason Jina beats Cohere Rerank-3 on Chinese/English mixed corpora), and how Jina compares to OpenAI text-embedding-3-large, Voyage AI, Cohere Embed v3, and the OpenRouter embeddings router.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The headline numbers for a team comparing Jina against the alternatives in 2026: jina-embeddings-v3 at <code>$0.02/M tokens</code> with 10K free tokens/month, jina-reranker-v3 at <code>$0.018/M tokens</code>, Reader API at <code>$0.02/M tokens</code> with the first 1M tokens free, and CLIP-v1 multimodal at <code>$0.02/M tokens</code>. Jina is the only major embedding provider that gives you a permanent free tier without a credit card, and the only one whose embeddings natively support 8K context with Matryoshka representation learning — which means you can store the same vector at 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 768, or 1024 dimensions and trade recall for storage at query time without re-embedding your corpus.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What is Jina AI, and why do the embeddings/reranker/reader APIs matter in 2026?</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Jina AI is a Berlin-based AI lab founded in 2020 by Han Xiao and Michael Günther, focused exclusively on what they call "the foundation models for multimodal AI" — the building blocks (embeddings, rerankers, readers) that sit between a raw corpus and a downstream LLM. As of June 2026, the company has raised $125M across three rounds (the Series A2 of $50M was led by Millennium New Horizons in October 2025), and the Jina Embeddings v3 model is currently the most-downloaded open-source embedding on Hugging Face with 4.2M+ monthly downloads.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">What separates Jina from a pure LLM API like OpenAI or Anthropic is the <strong>scope</strong>: Jina ships no chat model, no image generator, no code completion. The only things Jina makes are embeddings (text and multimodal), rerankers, and the Reader API that fetches and cleans web content. That singular focus is why the Jina embeddings v3 model has a longer context window (8192 tokens vs OpenAI's 8191) and a more aggressive Matryoshka implementation than any of the closed competitors, and why the Jina reranker beats Cohere Rerank-3 on multilingual benchmarks by 6-11% nDCG@10.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For a team building RAG in 2026, the three APIs together solve the canonical production problem in a way that no other single-vendor stack does:</p>

<ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-700 my-4 space-y-1">

<li><strong>jina-embeddings-v3</strong> (570M params, 8K context, Matryoshka) — the embedding step that converts your chunks into a vector for the vector store</li>

<li><strong>jina-reranker-v3</strong> (multilingual, listwise reranking) — the reranking step that re-orders the top-K candidates from the vector store before passing them to the LLM</li>

<li><strong>Reader API</strong> (URL → clean Markdown) — the data-collection step that fetches and cleans external content before embedding</li>

</ol>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The economic argument for Jina in 2026 is as strong as the technical one. At <code>$0.02/M tokens</code>, jina-embeddings-v3 is roughly 60% cheaper than OpenAI text-embedding-3-large ($0.13/M) and 80% cheaper than Cohere Embed v3 ($0.10/M) for the same 1024-dimension output. With the 50% batch discount, the effective rate drops to <code>$0.01/M tokens</code>, which is the cheapest production-quality embedding API in the market. The 10K tokens/month free tier is permanent and requires no credit card, which makes Jina the default starting point for prototypes and side projects — and the natural upgrade path when the prototype turns into production.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Jina AI endpoint catalog in 2026</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Jina exposes nine production endpoints as of July 2026, organized along the RAG pipeline from data collection to retrieval to reranking. The endpoint surface is deliberately narrow compared to a model aggregator like OpenRouter or a multimodal platform like Replicate — Jina ships embedding, reranking, reader, and CLIP, and nothing else. This is part of why the Jina APIs are the cleanest path to high-quality RAG primitives: there is no "long tail" of half-supported models to navigate.</p>

<table class="w-full text-sm text-gray-700 my-6 border-collapse border border-gray-300">

<thead class="bg-gray-100">

<tr><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Endpoint</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Model</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Output dim</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Context</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Per-M price</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Use case</th></tr>

