<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The price anchor in 2026 is the comparison to OpenAI text-embedding-3-large. OpenAI charges <code>$0.13/M tokens</code> for 3072-dim output (or $0.13/M with a 256-dim Matryoshka-shortened output, which OpenAI also supports but only for the 3-large model). Jina's v3 at $0.02/M is <strong>6.5x cheaper</strong> at the same 1024-dim output. For a team embedding 100M tokens/month (a moderate production RAG workload), Jina costs $2,000/year; OpenAI costs $13,000/year for the same volume. The free tier is sufficient for prototypes and side projects up to 10K tokens/month — most weekend hackathon projects never exceed this.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For volume customers, Jina offers a custom Enterprise contract starting at $50K/year annual commit. The Enterprise tier includes dedicated inference capacity (no rate limit contention with public traffic), private VPC deployment (the model runs in the customer's AWS account, billed via the AWS Marketplace), and a 99.95% uptime SLA. For most teams, however, the standard tier with rate limit auto-scaling is sufficient up to ~1B tokens/month.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">jina-embeddings-v3 in detail: 8K context + Matryoshka + task-specific LoRA</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">jina-embeddings-v3 is the flagship embedding model and the reason most teams adopt Jina in the first place. Released in July 2024, v3 is a 570M-parameter text encoder trained on 100+ languages with three production-relevant innovations that no other embedding provider ships in a single model:</p>

<ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-700 my-4 space-y-1">

<li><strong>Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL)</strong> — the embedding output is designed to be truncatable to any of {32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 768, 1024} dimensions with minimal accuracy loss. Storage cost goes from 3072 dims to 256 dims = 12x reduction, at the cost of 1-3% recall. The same vector can be re-fetched at a higher dimension later without re-embedding.</li>

<li><strong>8K context window</strong> — 8192 tokens of input, vs OpenAI text-embedding-3-large's 8191 and Cohere Embed v3's 512. This means fewer chunks for long-document RAG (a 30-page contract can be one or two chunks instead of 30+).</li>

<li><strong>Task-specific LoRA adapters</strong> — at inference time, you pass an <code>task</code> parameter (<code>retrieval.query</code>, <code>retrieval.passage</code>, <code>classification</code>, <code>separation</code>, <code>text-matching</code>) and the model applies a different LoRA adapter for each task. The <code>retrieval.query</code> vs <code>retrieval.passage</code> distinction alone adds 4-7% nDCG@10 vs single-mode embeddings.</li>

</ol>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The practical workflow for a team adopting jina-embeddings-v3 is:</p>

<pre class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-md p-4 my-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gray-800"&gt;# Install the SDK
pip install jina-ai

&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;Embed a query (retrieval mode)&lt;/h1&gt;
from jina import Client
client = Client(api_key="jina_...")

&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;Query side&lt;/h1&gt;
query_vec = client.embeddings.create(
    model="jina-embeddings-v3",
    input=["What is Matryoshka representation learning?"],
    task="retrieval.query",
    dimensions=512,  # truncated via Matryoshka
    late_chunking=True
)

&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;Corpus side&lt;/h1&gt;
doc_vecs = client.embeddings.create(
    model="jina-embeddings-v3",
    input=["Matryoshka representation learning...", "BERT is a transformer..."],
    task="retrieval.passage",
    dimensions=512
)

&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;Cosine similarity search&lt;/h1&gt;
import numpy as np
scores = np.dot(query_vec.data[0].embedding, np.array(doc_vecs.data[0].embedding).T)
&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;scores[0] will be highest (the relevant doc)</code></pre></h1>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The <code>late_chunking=True</code> parameter is the most under-used feature in 2026. When set, the model applies the embedding model to the full document first, then chunks the resulting contextualized token embeddings, instead of the usual "chunk first, embed second" pipeline. This preserves long-range context across chunk boundaries and gives 5-9% nDCG@10 improvement on long-document benchmarks (HotpotQA, NarrativeQA). For a team building RAG over PDFs longer than 20 pages, late_chunking is the single highest-ROI feature in the Jina SDK.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">jina-reranker-v3: multilingual reranking for production RAG</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">A vector search returns the top-K candidates by cosine similarity, but cosine similarity is a noisy proxy for relevance — the first-stage retriever is recall-oriented by design, and the second-stage reranker is where you trade compute for precision. jina-reranker-v3 is the second-stage reranker, and it is the strongest multilingual reranker in production as of 2026, beating Cohere Rerank-3 by 6-11% nDCG@10 on Chinese/English mixed corpora and by 3-7% on European-language corpora.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The technical story behind v3 is the move from pointwise reranking (the older v2 series) to listwise reranking. Pointwise rerankers score each (query, document) pair independently, which means they cannot take advantage of relative ranking signals — a document that is highly relevant in isolation but only the 5th best fit for a query is still scored by the same calibration. Listwise rerankers see the full top-K list and produce a calibrated ranking, which is the right output for the next-stage LLM prompt.</p>

