<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Reader API + CLIP-v1: data collection and multimodal retrieval</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The Reader API is the Jina endpoint that solves the data-collection problem for RAG: it takes a URL and returns clean, well-formatted Markdown with all the boilerplate (nav, footer, ads, cookie banners) stripped. A single API call replaces the usual Playwright + BeautifulSoup + custom-cleaner pipeline, and the first 1M tokens/month are free.</p>

<pre class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-md p-4 my-4 overflow-x-auto"><code class="text-sm text-gray-800"&gt;import requests

&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;Reader API: URL → clean Markdown&lt;/h1&gt;
resp = requests.get(
    "https://r.jina.ai/https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04594",
    headers=&#123;"Authorization": "Bearer jina_..."&#125;
)
markdown = resp.text
&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;Returns clean Markdown with title, abstract, headings, and&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4"&gt;body text. No &lt;script&gt;, no &lt;nav&gt;, no ads, no cookie banners.</code></pre></h1>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">The Reader API is not a headless browser — it is an HTTP fetcher with a custom HTML-to-Markdown converter. This means it cannot execute JavaScript, and SPA-rendered pages (e.g., a React app) will return an empty body. For SPA pages, the recommended pattern is to either (a) use Reader API with the <code>x-target-elements=html,body</code> and <code>x-wait-for-selector</code> headers to give the page time to render on the server side, or (b) fall back to a Playwright/Puppeteer pipeline. For 80% of RAG use cases (blogs, docs, news, Wikipedia, product pages), the Reader API works out of the box.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">jina-clip-v1 is the multimodal embedding endpoint for text-image cross-modal retrieval. Trained on 446M image-text pairs across 89 languages, CLIP-v1 produces a 768-dim embedding for both text and image inputs in the same vector space, which means a query like "red running shoes" can return a product photo as a result. The use cases are e-commerce visual search, content moderation image matching, and any retrieval task where the corpus is mixed text+image. CLIP-v1 at $0.02/M tokens is 5-10x cheaper than the Cohere multimodal endpoint (now in private beta).</p>

